---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { defaultEmailClients, emailTestingClients, emailTestingDescription, sampleEmail } from "../lib/email-testing";
import { engineVersion } from "../lib/playground";
import clientsPackage from "../../../../packages/email-clients/package.json";
import "../styles/email-testing.css";

const cli = `npx @hyper-elements/cli check email.html --json
npx @hyper-elements/cli preview email.html`;
const library = `import { mkdir, readFile, writeFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { renderInEmailClients } from "@hyper-elements/email-clients";

const html = await readFile("email.html", "utf8");
const { previews, diagnostics } = await renderInEmailClients(html, {
  emailClients: ["outlook", "gmail", "apple-mail"],
});

await mkdir("out", { recursive: true });
for (const preview of previews) {
  await writeFile(\`out/\${preview.filename}\`, preview.html);
}
console.log(diagnostics);`;
const agent = `await window.hyperElementsEmailTesting.check({
  html: "<!doctype html><html><body>…</body></html>",
  clients: ["outlook", "gmail", "apple-mail"],
  dark: false,
});`;
---
<Base title="Test HTML emails across email clients" description={emailTestingDescription}>
  <section class="et-hero wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Email testing · by Hyper Elements</p>
    <h1>Test your HTML email.<br /><span class="grad">See it across clients.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">Check HTML email compatibility and preview Outlook, Gmail, Apple Mail, Yahoo Mail and AOL Mail locally. Use an email from any builder, MJML, React Email, or your own HTML.</p>
    <div class="et-actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#playground">Test your HTML <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#use-in-code">Use the library or CLI</a>
    </div>
    <p class="et-facts">Free · No account · No React required · No test send</p>
  </section>

  <section id="playground" class="wrap et-workspace" aria-labelledby="playground-title" data-email-testing data-runtime-version={`${engineVersion}-${clientsPackage.version}-html-1`}>
    <div class="et-section-heading">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Try it here</p><h2 id="playground-title">The email testing playground</h2></div>
      <a class="et-doc-link" href="/docs/email-client-emulator">Client coverage &amp; documentation ↗</a>
    </div>
    <p class="et-intro">Paste a complete HTML email or open an .html file. Your HTML stays in this tab. Referenced images and fonts may load from their hosts.</p>
    <noscript><p class="et-notice">Enable JavaScript to use the playground, or use the library and CLI commands below.</p></noscript>
    <div class="et-bench">
      <div class="et-input">
        <div class="et-toolbar">
          <label for="email-html">Your email HTML</label>
          <div class="et-tools">
            <button type="button" id="email-example" disabled>Example</button>
            <button type="button" id="email-open" disabled>Open .html</button>
            <input id="email-file" type="file" accept=".html,.htm,text/html" hidden />
          </div>
        </div>
        <textarea id="email-html" aria-describedby="email-input-help" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocomplete="off" wrap="off">{sampleEmail}</textarea>
        <p id="email-input-help" class="et-input-help">Complete document, including &lt;html&gt; and &lt;body&gt; · Up to 250 KB</p>
        <fieldset class="et-clients">
          <legend>Preview in</legend>
          <div>{emailTestingClients.map((client) => <label><input type="checkbox" name="email-client" value={client.id} checked={defaultEmailClients.includes(client.id)} /><span>{client.name}</span></label>)}</div>
        </fieldset>
        <div class="et-run-bar">
          <label class="et-dark"><input id="email-dark" type="checkbox" /> Include dark mode</label>
          <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button" id="email-run" disabled>Test email <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="et-output">
        <div class="et-toolbar">
          <span>Client preview</span>
          <button type="button" id="email-size" disabled>Actual size</button>
        </div>
        <div class="et-preview-controls" hidden id="email-preview-controls">
          <label class="et-sr-only" for="email-preview">Choose a client preview</label>
          <select id="email-preview"></select>
          <span id="email-preview-note"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="et-empty" id="email-empty">
          <span class="et-envelope" aria-hidden="true">✉</span>
          <h3>Same HTML. Different clients.</h3>
          <p>Test the example to see how Classic Outlook handles rounded corners, then try your own email.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="et-viewport" id="email-viewport" hidden><div id="email-sheet"></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="et-status-bar"><p id="email-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Ready when you are.</p><button type="button" class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm" id="email-download" disabled>Download report</button></div>
    <p id="email-error" class="et-notice" role="alert" hidden></p>
    <section class="et-findings" id="email-findings" aria-labelledby="findings-title" hidden>
      <h3 id="findings-title">Compatibility findings</h3>
      <p id="email-findings-summary"></p>
      <ul id="email-diagnostics"></ul>
      <ul id="email-skipped" class="et-skipped"></ul>
    </section>
    <p class="et-limit">These are modeled client previews, with compatibility diagnostics. Some client variants are approximate; a clean report does not guarantee an identical render in every inbox. <a href="/docs/email-client-emulator#checks-and-the-emulator">Read how the checks and emulator work.</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap et-details" id="use-in-code" aria-labelledby="code-title">
    <div class="et-section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">For your workflow</p><h2 id="code-title">The same checks, in your code.</h2></div></div>
    <p class="lede">Keep the email you already have. The JavaScript and TypeScript library takes complete HTML, and the CLI checks a file directly. Neither needs React for HTML input.</p>
    <div class="et-recipes">
      <article><h3>From the terminal or an agent</h3><p>Check compatibility and readability, then write the client previews. The CLI adds lint checks to the client diagnostics shown here.</p><CodeBlock code={cli} lang="bash" file="Terminal" /><p>Open the generated files under <code>out/clients/email/</code> in Chromium or Chrome. For browser findings and screenshots, follow the <a href="/docs/cli#preview">CLI browser setup</a>.</p></article>
      <article><h3>From JavaScript or TypeScript</h3><p>Install <code>@hyper-elements/email-clients</code>, save this as <code>preview-email.mjs</code>, and run <code>node preview-email.mjs</code>.</p><CodeBlock code={library} lang="js" file="preview-email.mjs" /><p>Open <code>out/outlook.html</code>. See the <a href="/docs/email-client-emulator#test-any-html-email">HTML email API</a> for all options.</p></article>
    </div>
    <details class="et-agent"><summary>Use this playground from a browser agent</summary><p>The page exposes <code>window.hyperElementsEmailTesting.check()</code> and, in browsers that support WebMCP, the <code>check_html_email</code> tool. Both update the playground and return diagnostics, preview metadata, and skipped variants as JSON. <code>ok</code> means previews were generated; inspect the diagnostics and approximate flags before judging compatibility.</p><CodeBlock code={agent} lang="js" /><p>The <code>clients</code> IDs are <code>outlook</code>, <code>outlook-com</code>, <code>gmail</code>, <code>gmail-app</code>, <code>apple-mail</code>, <code>yahoo</code>, and <code>aol</code>. Omit them to use Outlook, Gmail and Apple Mail. HTML is processed locally and is not saved as a draft.</p></details>
  </section>
  <script>
    import "../email-testing/main";
  </script>
</Base>
